insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,category,fact,source_note) values
('1 Kings 16:29','scholarly_agreement','The reign of Ahab belongs to the Omride dynasty, remembered in Assyrian and Moabite inscriptions. These records establish real rulers and political connections, but do not narrate every event in Kings.','1 Kings 16:28–34; Mesha Stele and Assyrian royal inscriptions; Answers in Genesis archaeology https://answersingenesis.org/archaeology/6-archaeological-finds/.'),
('2 Kings 3:16','plain_language','When Elisha says ''make this valley full of ditches,'' imagine digging channels or trenches to catch and direct water. The promise in the story arrives without the usual sound of wind or rain, so the preparation seems surprising.','2 Kings 3:16–20; compare translations ''ditches'' and ''pools''.'),
('Isaiah 1:18','plain_language','''Though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow'' uses contrasting colors to make forgiveness memorable. Scarlet was a vivid red dye; the picture is about cleansing, not a promise to change someone''s clothes.','Isaiah 1:18; ancient color and dye imagery.'),
('Isaiah 40:6','ancient_culture','In a dry climate, grass and wildflowers can appear after rain and wither quickly when heat returns. Isaiah uses that familiar sight to contrast fragile human life with the lasting word of God.','Isaiah 40:6–8; Levantine seasonal climate and prophetic poetry.'),
('John 18:38','scholarly_debate','Pilate''s short question, ''What is truth?'', has been read as skeptical, sarcastic, or dismissive. The Greek text does not tell us his tone of voice, so scholars and readers disagree on what exactly he meant by asking it.','John 18:37–38; interpretive debate, no surviving recording of Pilate''s tone.'),
('Mark 13:32','scholarly_debate','Jesus says no one knows the day or hour, not even the angels, and names the Son in many manuscripts. Scholars examine this statement when discussing the incarnation and how Mark''s Gospel presents Jesus'' knowledge; traditions interpret it differently.','Mark 13:32; Greek manuscript evidence and Christian christological interpretation.'),
('Matthew 14:24','plain_language','A boat described as ''buffeted by the waves'' was being pushed and battered by rough water. The disciples were struggling against a contrary wind, meaning the wind was blowing against their desired direction.','Matthew 14:24–25; compare KJV ''tossed with waves'' and modern translations.'),
('Matthew 26:63','ancient_culture','The high priest''s demand that Jesus answer under oath belonged to a legal and religious setting in which solemn words before God carried special seriousness. The scene is more like formal questioning than a casual conversation.','Matthew 26:62–66; Second Temple Jewish legal and religious contexts; procedures in the narrative are historically debated.')
